WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.580
- The plucky YouTubers
behind the channel MxR Plays,

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:05.370
claim that they're being
extorted by Jukin Media,

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:08.830
all for uploading some
reaction videos to YouTube.

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:10.310
On the other hand, Jukin says

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:12.540
that MxR committed copyright infringement

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.090
by using their videos without permission.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:15.923
Who's right?

00:00:15.923 --> 00:00:18.850
Well, I have some good news
and some bad news for MxR.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.270
The good news is that
they might have a defense

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:22.740
to Jukin's claims.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.530
The bad news is that they
might not have a defense.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:26.960
I have worse news still,

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.110
they might not just be
liable for the $6,000

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:34.003
that Jukin demands, but
actually $600,000 or more.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.691
And the worst news of all,

00:00:36.691 --> 00:00:39.440
there's no way to figure
out which one is right

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.930
until they go to court
and spend potentially

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.150
a million dollars on attorneys fees.

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:47.704
So who's right in this whole
reaction video debacle?

00:00:47.704 --> 00:00:49.473
Well, let's think like a lawyer.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.520
Hey, Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.500
because I've received
hundreds of emails and DMs

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.720
about this particular case
and this particular video.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.070
This case has it all.

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:04.750
A little guy who's getting pushed around,

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:06.507
a bad guy who might be a troll,

00:01:06.507 --> 00:01:09.670
the intricacies of
copyright law, huge stakes

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.670
and thousands of teenage legal experts.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:14.810
So let me give you a
quick summary of the facts

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:15.700
as I understand them,

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:17.020
because they're going to be necessary

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.200
to make any kind of legal judgment here.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.980
A few days ago, the
YouTube channel, MxR Plays,

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:23.560
posted a video to YouTube claiming

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:24.850
that they were being extorted.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:27.088
- Something called the Jukin Media.

00:01:27.088 --> 00:01:29.020
It feels kind of weird talking about it

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:30.070
'cause it just happened.

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:32.120
- Apparently, a few weeks or months ago,

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:35.130
they have been posting various reactions

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:36.010
on their channel.

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:39.540
They would take viral and meme video clips

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.660
from the internet and the two of them,

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:44.550
I assume that they are a
boyfriend and girlfriend couple,

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:47.130
would react to them in some ways.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:50.050
Sometimes it would be the
man that was responding

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:50.930
and reacting to the clip.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.160
Sometimes it was the woman in MxR,

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.860
not unlike many YouTube channels

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:57.360
that are out there
reacting to various clips.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.040
Now as it happens, a
company called Jukin Media,

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:04.060
claims that the couple used
clips from Jukin database.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:06.660
And Jukin appears to
be sort of a middleman

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.610
that goes about clearing
rights to various viral clips.

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:11.810
If they see that a video is trending,

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:13.770
they'll go out and they'll
reach out to the person

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:14.750
that created the clip

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.020
and they will get the ownership rights

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:17.930
to these various clips

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:20.350
so that they can license
those clips to other people.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:22.450
- It used to be that they
would claim your videos

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.630
and take the revenue
from that video, right?

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:26.932
- Better than a strike.

00:02:26.932 --> 00:02:29.610
- And so we started, you know,

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:31.323
removing their clips from our videos.

00:02:31.323 --> 00:02:32.940
- Of course, because we're like,

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.524
okay, that's your clip,
so we won't use it.

00:02:35.524 --> 00:02:38.300
- So basically, they just
strike our channel now,

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.530
whenever they see a clip.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:41.520
- Some call Jukin Media a middleman,

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.290
they themselves analogize to Shutterstock,

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.780
where they buy rights to
various forms of media

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:47.470
and then sell it to other third parties.

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:49.900
Generally, you can buy a license
from Jukin on their website

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:51.984
to use their clips for $49 per clip.

00:02:51.984 --> 00:02:55.010
Some called Jukin Media a copyright troll,

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.750
they specifically get viral clips

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:59.400
so that they can extort money
from other third parties.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:00.670
I won't make a judgment call

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:03.180
as to whether they are doing
a good thing or a bad thing

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.013
for the internet.

00:03:04.013 --> 00:03:05.100
But in this particular case,

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.737
it appears that MxR admit
they have used various clips

00:03:08.737 --> 00:03:10.340
that belong to Jukin.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.650
I don't know if you can actually owns

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:12.770
the rights to these clips.

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:13.700
But for the sake of argument,

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:15.950
let's assume that Jukin does in fact own

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:18.100
the copyright to these various clips.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:21.150
In the past, Jukin has reached out to MxR

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:24.200
and they have demanded
$2,000 from the couple

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.970
for the couple's use
of various Jukin clips

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:28.520
in their YouTube channel.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.549
- They email us with the bill

00:03:30.549 --> 00:03:35.549
and they charged us $1,500 per
clip, that was in our videos.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:41.650
And so today we got hit with a
huge bill of $6,000 in total.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:44.833
- Jukin can came back
and demanded $6,000 more

00:03:44.833 --> 00:03:46.910
for the use of four other clips

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:49.130
they didn't previously make a demand on.

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:51.870
It was at that point that
MxR went to the internet

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:54.270
and as one does when they
have a YouTube channel,

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:55.530
posted a video saying

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:59.080
that they were being extorted
by Julian for $6,000.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:03.090
- So we've paid about $2,000 in total,

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:05.720
and now we have another $6,000 to pay.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:07.510
And if you don't pay,

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:10.390
then basically they'll
start striking your channel.

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:12.850
- MxR claims that their
use of these videos

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:13.790
is fair use, of course,

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:16.470
and Jukin disputes that characterization.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.890
The stakes here are incredibly
high in several respects.

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:21.240
Number one, Jukin is asking for $6,000,

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:22.510
which for many households,

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:25.050
that amount of money could
lead to financial ruin.

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.867
But additionally, when you
get attorneys involved,

00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:30.020
$6,000 is a drop in the bucket

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:30.853
and we can be talking

00:04:30.853 --> 00:04:32.300
about hundreds of
thousands of dollars here.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.511
But on top of that, still,

00:04:34.511 --> 00:04:35.790
we're talking about copyright strikes here

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:37.230
and Jukin has threatened

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:39.640
to issue copyright strikes against MxR,

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:41.940
which means that with
three copyright strikes,

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:46.100
potentially MxR's entire channel
could get deleted forever.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.730
- Basically go through
an entire video library,

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:51.980
most likely find is
three instances of some,

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:53.170
you know, random clip,

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:56.150
and then they'll basically
remove our channel

00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:57.620
if we don't pay them.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:59.630
- And we don't really
have power against it.

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:01.500
We're stuck between a
hard place and a rock,

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.670
so I feel like they're just
constantly gonna extort us.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.210
- So the stakes are incredibly high,

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:08.520
which by the way, is
why I created a course

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:10.890
for creators called copyrightcourse.com,

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:12.720
to deal with this exact situation

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.870
of where you are threatened
with copyright strikes

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:16.720
and what to do if you do
get a copyright strike

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:17.553
on your channel.

00:05:17.553 --> 00:05:19.630
So let's talk about this exact situation

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:22.330
and the law that governs
this potential dispute.

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:24.450
Now, first a disclaimer, I
don't know all the facts,

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:26.240
I've gotten some of the facts from MxR,

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:27.680
which are going to be necessarily bias.

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:29.120
I've gotten some of the facts from Jukin,

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:31.410
which are also going to
be necessarily biased.

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:32.800
I don't know all the facts,

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:34.460
but one of the good
things about copyright law

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:35.920
is that often it just depends

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:37.850
on the underlying works themselves,

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:40.830
which we can look at and we can analyze.

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:43.320
So let's talk about
copyright law in general,

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.830
and specifically the copyrights.

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:47.630
One of the biggest myths on the internet

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:49.210
is that if something is out there,

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:51.390
you can use it for almost any purpose.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:53.270
That's absolutely not the case.

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:55.810
If there is a clip on the
internet, someone owns it,

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:58.050
it might be hard to track them down.

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:01.034
But that clip, whether
it's a video, a sound file

00:06:01.034 --> 00:06:04.290
or a picture, they belong to someone.

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:06.060
And the default position of copyright law

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:08.268
is that you need permission, a license,

00:06:08.268 --> 00:06:10.980
to be able to use that underlying work,

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.920
whether it's a picture or
a video or what have you.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:15.910
However, there's an exception
to that general rule,

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:19.230
that if you use that
media in a fair use way,

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:20.590
you don't need to get permission,

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:23.220
and you also don't have
to pay a license fee.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:25.600
But if it's not fair
use, you do have to pay

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:27.330
and the rights holder can block you

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:29.950
from actually using that underlying media.

00:06:29.950 --> 00:06:31.834
Fair Use is incredibly complex,

00:06:31.834 --> 00:06:33.900
even judges get this wrong

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:37.120
and often judges say this is
the most complicated portion

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:39.560
of copyright law in all of jurisprudence.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:41.490
But if you go to 17 USC 107,

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:43.870
you will find the Fair Use factors

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:46.750
to determine whether you're
using something in fair use.

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:48.880
The Fair Use clause states
that notwithstanding

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:49.770
the rest of the copyright act,

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:51.490
for purposes such as criticism, comment,

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:54.663
news reporting, teaching,
scholarship or research

00:06:54.663 --> 00:06:56.930
is not an infringement of copyright.

00:06:56.930 --> 00:06:59.930
And in determining whether
the use made of a work

00:06:59.930 --> 00:07:01.910
in particular case is fair use,

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:04.380
the factors to be considered
shall include one,

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:06.330
the purpose and character of the use.

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:08.400
Two, the nature of the copyrighted work.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.780
Three, the amount and
substantiality of the portion used

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:13.420
and four, the effect
upon the potential market

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:15.390
or value of the copyrighted work.

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:17.280
So let's talk about that really quickly.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:19.140
So the first part of this section

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:20.890
of the Copyright Act says that,

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:23.950
generally things that are
for the purpose of commentary

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:26.910
and criticism can be considered fair use.

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:28.500
Now that's not dispositive.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.060
But in order to get to
the rest of the factors,

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:33.570
you generally need a purpose

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:36.410
that is recognized by
the Fair Use statute.

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:38.970
Now that's not an exclusive
list of the things

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:40.360
that qualify as fair use.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:41.810
But for intensive purposes,

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:43.540
let's assume that that's
the entire universe.

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:45.570
Now, there are four factors to determine

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:48.840
whether the actual use itself is fair use.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:50.950
Now these are factors,
they're not elements,

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:52.290
you can't just check them off

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:54.500
and you have to balance
every single one of them.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:56.440
Just because you win one factor,

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:59.610
doesn't mean that the use
is going to be fair use,

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:01.330
you have to balance all four together.

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:03.560
And the first factor is
by far the most important.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:05.580
This is going to be a
gross oversimplification

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:07.940
because there are literally
millions of pages written

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:10.100
about fair use in American jurisprudence.

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:12.060
A quick summary of the first factor

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.930
is that this looks to the
transformativeness of the use,

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:19.190
assuming your purpose is
recognized by the Fair Use statute.

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:20.700
Let's assume commentary and criticism

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:25.510
because that's what most YouTube
use of media falls under.

00:08:25.510 --> 00:08:26.500
Sometimes it's parody,

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:28.360
but usually it's commentary and criticism.

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:30.600
The first factor looks at
whether you have transformed

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:33.670
the purpose of the original clip itself.

00:08:33.670 --> 00:08:35.210
So for example, a movie review.

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:37.930
A movie review often
uses clips from the movie

00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:39.280
that it is reviewing,

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:41.210
but it would be very, very difficult

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:44.332
to be able to have a coherent criticism

00:08:44.332 --> 00:08:47.150
or to be able to comment on a movie

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:49.400
without being able to use small portions

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:51.410
of the movie itself.

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:55.250
But how does a movie review
transform the underlying movie?

00:08:55.250 --> 00:08:57.220
The purpose of a movie generally speaking

00:08:57.220 --> 00:08:58.760
is for the entertainment value.

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:01.190
You watch a movie because
you're there to be entertained.

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:02.805
But a movie review itself

00:09:02.805 --> 00:09:05.230
is not for the purpose of entertainment,

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:08.580
you're not usurping the
original purpose of the movie,

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:09.950
you are making a movie review

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:12.750
specifically to create
commentary and criticism

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:13.780
about that movie.

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:15.200
That is what we're talking about

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:18.620
when we're talking about
transformative nature of fair use.

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:20.850
And that's why the Copyright
Act specifically carves out,

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:23.750
commentary and criticism as a fair use.

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:25.340
So that is the fair use purpose,

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:27.710
plus the character of the
use, which is transformative.

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:29.920
The first factor is by
far the most important

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:31.370
and you can overcome a lot of sins

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:32.410
and the other three factors

00:09:32.410 --> 00:09:35.270
if you have a highly
transformative use case

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:39.071
with a clearly fair use
commentary and criticism purpose

00:09:39.071 --> 00:09:40.790
in your use.

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:42.410
So that takes me to the second factor,

00:09:42.410 --> 00:09:44.050
the nature of the copyrighted work.

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:46.750
This generally looks to whether
the underlying work itself

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:49.610
is factual or fictional in nature.

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:52.760
Copyright doesn't want to give
people a monopoly over facts.

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:55.390
So as a result, factual works,

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:57.720
the things that actually
happened in real life,

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:00.360
tend to get less protection
than things that are fictional

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:01.630
and highly creative.

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:04.030
This tends to be actually
the least important

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:05.340
of the four factors.

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:06.830
Factor number three looks to the amount

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:08.470
and substantiality of the portion used

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:10.830
in relation to the
copyrighted work as a whole.

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:12.900
This is often summarized
by saying that people

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:15.650
are only allowed to use the
minimum amount necessary,

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:16.810
the bare minimum,

00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:20.012
to be able to get their
commentary or criticism across.

00:10:20.012 --> 00:10:22.990
So just because you're giving
commentary or criticism

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:24.140
doesn't mean you're allowed to use

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:27.070
the entire portion of the underlying work.

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:29.360
Sometimes you are, sometimes
the clips are very short,

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:30.770
sometimes you're only
dealing with the picture

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:33.240
which you kind of have
to use the whole thing.

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:34.073
But the bottom line is

00:10:34.073 --> 00:10:36.650
that you generally can't
use the entire work itself

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:39.583
or you can't use the
heart of the work itself

00:10:39.583 --> 00:10:42.777
in order to form your
commentary or criticism.

00:10:42.777 --> 00:10:45.410
And finally, that takes
me to factor number four,

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:47.810
the effect of the use
upon the potential market

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:49.040
or value of the copyrighted work.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:51.210
This is actually pretty
similar to the third factor.

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:53.980
The idea is that we don't
want people using things

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:57.950
in a fair use way that
usurps the entire market

00:10:57.950 --> 00:10:59.230
for the original work.

00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:00.880
If people are gonna watch your video

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:03.040
and not watch the original video

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:05.550
or not pay a license fee to the original,

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:07.000
then that's a factor that cuts

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:08.987
against your claim of fair use.

00:11:08.987 --> 00:11:11.610
And it also kind of overlaps
with the first factor as well.

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:13.960
Because the less transformative your use,

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:17.100
the more likely you are to
use the original purpose

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:18.350
of the underlying work

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:21.910
and the more likely you are
to usurp the potential market

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:23.100
for the underlying work

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:26.350
and degrade the value of
that copyrighted work.

00:11:26.350 --> 00:11:29.630
Now, if this all sounds
incredibly squishy and ambiguous,

00:11:29.630 --> 00:11:31.190
you are entirely right.

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:32.680
That's sort of a feature not a bug though.

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:34.900
The purpose of fair use is that

00:11:34.900 --> 00:11:37.880
this ambiguity and the
fact that it's squishy,

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:39.020
leads to flexibility,

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:43.180
so we can carve fair use into
whatever we need it to be

00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:45.390
and we can apply it to new situations.

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:46.380
It's quite flexible.

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:48.460
The problem is often
you have to go to court

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:50.200
to be able to adjudicate these things.

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:53.260
And it also means that there
are absolutely no absolutes.

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:57.030
You can't say that all
reaction videos are fair use,

00:11:57.030 --> 00:11:58.910
some are and some aren't,

00:11:58.910 --> 00:12:02.020
it depends on the facts
of the particular case.

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:04.360
So now that you have a
reasonably good understanding

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:06.670
of what fair use means and its limitations

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:07.840
and its ambiguity,

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:11.450
let's look at the videos
that MxR actually used,

00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:13.640
and which Jukin claims that MxR

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:16.460
now owes them thousands of
dollars for having used.

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:19.790
What MxR tends to do, is they
will take the general clip

00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:21.690
and then they will inset their own video

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:24.090
in a small portion of
the rest of the frame.

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:26.200
They'll play the entire clip it seems

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:27.720
and then they will react to it.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:30.720
So apparently Jukin
sent MxR a demand letter

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:33.330
that referenced for Jukin owned videos

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:36.180
that were used in three
MxR reaction videos.

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:40.580
On that basis, Jukin claim
that MxR owes them $6,000.

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:42.720
So let's look at these different videos.

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:44.390
So the first video that is at issue

00:12:44.390 --> 00:12:47.210
is them reacting to a
video of a cat sticking

00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:48.043
its tongue out.

00:12:48.043 --> 00:12:49.120
It's only a couple seconds long.

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:50.850
I'm gonna zoom in on MxR

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:53.570
because I don't want to
infringe the copyright

00:12:53.570 --> 00:12:55.473
of the cat video owner.

00:12:58.340 --> 00:12:59.890
- It made me forget about the mayonnaise

00:12:59.890 --> 00:13:01.970
and the maggots and the.

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:03.820
- So you can see it's
an incredibly short clip

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:07.350
and there's very minimal
commentary and criticism if any.

00:13:07.350 --> 00:13:08.700
Generally speaking in copyright law,

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:11.980
you have to comment and
criticize the actual clip itself.

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:14.570
You can't use the clip to
comment on something else.

00:13:14.570 --> 00:13:17.050
That's kind of the difference
between parody and satire.

00:13:17.050 --> 00:13:19.220
Parody comments on the underlying work,

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:21.330
satire comments on something else.

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:23.226
Parody is fair use often but

00:13:23.226 --> 00:13:25.720
satire is almost never fair use.

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:30.720
The next video is of MxR
reacting to a volcano exploding.

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:35.000
- Well that's.

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:36.750
Whoa, you see the shockwave?

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:38.290
- [Man] Watch out for the
shockwave it's coming.

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:39.140
- Did you see it?

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:43.859
Dude, that was awesome.

00:13:43.859 --> 00:13:44.692
Wasn't that awesome Jeannie?

00:13:44.692 --> 00:13:46.290
That was so awesome.

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:47.780
Do you want some water?

00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:48.990
- So you can see here again,

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:52.140
they're only playing a small
portion of this volcano video,

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:54.930
but arguably that's the
heart of the volcano video.

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:56.950
You're not gonna watch the 20 minutes

00:13:56.950 --> 00:13:58.640
of the volcano erupting.

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:00.630
The thing you want to see
is the volcano erupting.

00:14:00.630 --> 00:14:03.740
And the commentary, if any,
is basically that's cool,

00:14:03.740 --> 00:14:06.300
which is about as minimal as it gets.

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:08.240
Now in this third MxR video,

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:09.610
it appears that they've edited it

00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:11.480
to remove the Jukin videos.

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:13.100
I actually couldn't find the videos

00:14:13.100 --> 00:14:15.010
that Jukin references
in the demand letter.

00:14:15.010 --> 00:14:16.820
I wonder if they have edited those out

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:19.110
or maybe I just wasn't paying attention.

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:20.830
Jukin references a girl doing a back flip

00:14:20.830 --> 00:14:23.900
and guys lighting a
trail of leaves on fire.

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:26.240
I couldn't find that in
this underlying video.

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:27.890
But I do want to reference

00:14:27.890 --> 00:14:31.000
what MxR says at the very
beginning of this video.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:34.055
- So it's time once again
to get Jeannie to laugh.

00:14:34.055 --> 00:14:37.330
I've come over yet another
brilliant compilation

00:14:37.330 --> 00:14:40.290
of funny things all in the worthy endeavor

00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:43.220
of making my beautiful
darling girlfriend laugh

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:45.804
because she deserves happiness.

00:14:45.804 --> 00:14:46.780
- Sparkles.

00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:49.950
- They point out that
Henry is showing Jeannie

00:14:49.950 --> 00:14:51.490
these different videos

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:53.400
to see if he can get Jeannie to laugh.

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:56.840
In my estimation, that's not
really commentary or criticism.

00:14:56.840 --> 00:14:58.310
That's not a transformative purpose.

00:14:58.310 --> 00:15:01.840
And in fact, by showing those
clips in full to the audience,

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:03.740
arguably, they're using the clips

00:15:03.740 --> 00:15:06.740
for the exact same purpose
as they were created

00:15:06.740 --> 00:15:08.450
in the first place, to entertain people,

00:15:08.450 --> 00:15:09.960
to try and get people to laugh.

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:11.070
And it's entirely possible

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:14.170
that you might not watch the Jukin videos,

00:15:14.170 --> 00:15:18.000
because you saw basically the
whole thing in MxR's video.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:19.610
So some of those Fair Use factors

00:15:19.610 --> 00:15:23.120
might cut severely against
MxRs claim of fair use.

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:25.280
It's hard to say because
we don't have those clips

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:26.670
in this particular video.

00:15:26.670 --> 00:15:28.450
And there might be more or less commentary

00:15:28.450 --> 00:15:30.600
than in the prior two
videos that we looked at.

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:31.830
Now the last thing I wanna point out

00:15:31.830 --> 00:15:33.020
is that in the beginning of this video,

00:15:33.020 --> 00:15:35.500
Henry says that he's not going to display

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:38.230
the audio of a particular
clip, not a Jukin clip,

00:15:38.230 --> 00:15:39.690
I don't think but another one

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:42.230
because he's worried about
getting a copyright claim related

00:15:42.230 --> 00:15:43.070
to the music.

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:45.960
And that is bad when you're talking

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:48.130
about potential willful behavior,

00:15:48.130 --> 00:15:50.480
which is an issue when it comes to damages

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:51.910
that we'll talk about in a second.

00:15:51.910 --> 00:15:54.500
So there are a lot of bad
facts in this particular video.

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:57.270
In fact, some of these clips
might be dangerously close

00:15:57.270 --> 00:15:59.650
to a hypothetical that I
use when I give speeches

00:15:59.650 --> 00:16:02.480
about copyright and
explain things to creators.

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:04.793
An example of something that
you might think is fair use

00:16:04.793 --> 00:16:07.280
that is clearly not fair use.

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:08.810
The example that I come up with

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:10.232
and this is not an actual case,

00:16:10.232 --> 00:16:12.212
this is just me hypothesizing is that.

00:16:12.212 --> 00:16:16.180
We recognize that movie
reviews are often fair use,

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:18.140
often quintessential fair use.

00:16:18.140 --> 00:16:19.450
But imagine if you had a movie review

00:16:19.450 --> 00:16:22.407
that had the entire movie itself,

00:16:22.407 --> 00:16:25.110
the entire two hour long movie

00:16:25.110 --> 00:16:27.100
and then at the end of the movie,

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:29.100
the movie reviewer said,
that was pretty good,

00:16:29.100 --> 00:16:30.560
or I didn't really like that movie.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:32.410
You've got commentary and criticism,

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:35.020
you've got some sort of
criticism about the movie,

00:16:35.020 --> 00:16:36.430
it was good or it was bad.

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:39.042
But because you use the
entire movie itself,

00:16:39.042 --> 00:16:41.790
that's clearly not the minimum necessary

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:45.040
and the commentary itself is
also just about as minimal

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:46.070
as it gets.

00:16:46.070 --> 00:16:49.430
So the question of whether
MxR, Henry and Jeannie,

00:16:49.430 --> 00:16:52.680
are actually doing something
that is fair use commentary

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:55.100
and criticism or copyright infringement

00:16:55.100 --> 00:16:58.510
may turn on how courts have
looked at reaction videos

00:16:58.510 --> 00:16:59.610
in the past.

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:01.360
So let's look at some of those cases.

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:02.310
And this is hilarious,

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:05.210
because one of those cases
involves Jukin Media itself.

00:17:05.210 --> 00:17:06.043
Before we get to that,

00:17:06.043 --> 00:17:08.900
let's talk about my favorite
fair use case of all time

00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:11.480
and that is the h3h3Productions case.

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:14.470
In that particular case,
Hila and Ethan Kline,

00:17:14.470 --> 00:17:17.541
the comedy duo behind h3h3Productions,

00:17:17.541 --> 00:17:21.790
did a reaction video to a
video of a man named Matt Haas.

00:17:21.790 --> 00:17:23.427
It was something along the lines

00:17:23.427 --> 00:17:25.030
of park or girl versus workout guy,

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:26.560
which is a pretty cringe worthy thing

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:29.770
where a guy kind of stocks
a woman who's working out

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:30.850
on the street.

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:33.210
And in that video, they use almost all

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:34.530
of the entire clip itself,

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:37.870
but they are also peppering
the 10 minute long video

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:40.200
with lots and lots of commentary.

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:42.010
The Southern District of New York,

00:17:42.010 --> 00:17:44.520
which is in the news for
many different reasons,

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:47.310
sided with Hila and Ethan and said that

00:17:47.310 --> 00:17:49.850
this is a quintessential
commentary and criticism

00:17:49.850 --> 00:17:51.560
and quintessential fair use,

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:53.100
and so they threw the case out.

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:55.270
Now, it was a bit of a Pyrrhic victory

00:17:55.270 --> 00:17:58.710
because that required hundreds
of thousands of dollars

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:00.237
from Ethan and Hila Kline

00:18:00.237 --> 00:18:03.340
to be able to get to that
stage of adjudication,

00:18:03.340 --> 00:18:05.920
and that was taken out
in a very early stage,

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:08.530
on the motions, as we
say in the legal field,

00:18:08.530 --> 00:18:10.220
it didn't even go to trial.

00:18:10.220 --> 00:18:11.470
So if you go to trial,

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:14.280
you can expect to pay three
to four times as much.

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:16.920
I mean, honestly 800,000
to maybe a million dollars

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:18.610
to be able to adjudicate that.

00:18:18.610 --> 00:18:19.510
So that takes us to

00:18:19.510 --> 00:18:21.910
the Equals Three versus Jukin Media case,

00:18:21.910 --> 00:18:25.750
a case that actually
involves Jukin Media itself.

00:18:25.750 --> 00:18:27.250
This is a case that is pretty similar.

00:18:27.250 --> 00:18:30.880
You have a YouTube clip show
that is owned by Equals Three,

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:33.833
and they are reacting to a particular clip

00:18:33.833 --> 00:18:35.530
that is owned by Jukin.

00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:38.340
This case involved close
to 20 different clips,

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:40.360
but here's one that is sort of emblematic

00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:41.230
of the rest of them.

00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:43.520
Jukin owned a video of a bear getting

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:44.810
his head stuck in a bucket,

00:18:44.810 --> 00:18:46.870
and some construction
workers used to crane

00:18:46.870 --> 00:18:49.414
to get the bucket off of the bears head.

00:18:49.414 --> 00:18:53.538
The clip show used almost
all of the clip itself

00:18:53.538 --> 00:18:56.320
and then proceeded to talk about it

00:18:56.320 --> 00:18:58.280
for roughly about a minute.

00:18:58.280 --> 00:18:59.793
- Every time a bear gets his.

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:04.170
Winnie the Pooh, it just, it gets me.

00:19:04.170 --> 00:19:06.040
- And regarding this particular clip,

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:08.610
the court cited with Equals Three,

00:19:08.610 --> 00:19:11.394
and found that this was in fact fair use.

00:19:11.394 --> 00:19:12.870
If you were to ask me,

00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:15.870
what is the case that shows
the absolute bare minimum

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:18.420
of commentary necessary
to make a fair use claim?

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:20.940
I might point you to Equals
Three versus Jukin Media,

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:23.140
which is why I have cited to that case

00:19:23.140 --> 00:19:28.070
in almost all of my videos
that rely on fair use clips.

00:19:28.070 --> 00:19:29.680
But this case is a bit of a mixed bag.

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:31.260
The court decided as a matter of law

00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:33.210
that most of these clips were fair use,

00:19:33.210 --> 00:19:35.703
but also decided that some
could not be adjudicated,

00:19:35.703 --> 00:19:38.970
so those would have to
go to a jury to decide.

00:19:38.970 --> 00:19:41.440
And at that point, the
parties settled out of court.

00:19:41.440 --> 00:19:42.780
So it's a bit of a mixed bag

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:46.210
and Jukin might have been
right on some of those clips.

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:47.820
Now, if you want an in depth analysis

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:50.180
of the Equals Three
versus Jukin Media case,

00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:52.030
check out my friend
Leonard French's channel,

00:19:52.030 --> 00:19:53.880
he goes into a huge amount of detail

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:56.130
about this particular case exactly.

00:19:56.130 --> 00:19:57.410
The bottom line is that this would be

00:19:57.410 --> 00:19:59.207
an incredibly close call.

00:19:59.207 --> 00:20:02.140
MxR skating dangerously close to the line

00:20:02.140 --> 00:20:03.800
of it being not fair use.

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:05.640
But there's authority on both sides.

00:20:05.640 --> 00:20:07.120
And in fact, one of the cases

00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:09.070
that I would use against Jukin Nedia

00:20:09.070 --> 00:20:10.680
if I was representing MxR

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:13.400
is a case involving Jukin Media itself.

00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:15.360
But arguments can be made on both sides.

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:17.340
If I was representing MxR, which I'm not,

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:20.200
I'm not your attorney and
this is not legal advice.

00:20:20.200 --> 00:20:22.440
I would advise them not to go to court

00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:24.870
because I'd be worried that
number one, they would lose.

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:26.950
And even if they won, they would probably

00:20:26.950 --> 00:20:30.620
have to go all the way through
trial and take it to a jury,

00:20:30.620 --> 00:20:33.060
which would incur
potentially a million dollars

00:20:33.060 --> 00:20:34.040
in attorneys fees.

00:20:34.040 --> 00:20:36.534
And by the same token, stop admitting to.

00:20:36.534 --> 00:20:39.269
Stop going online and admitting
not only to liability,

00:20:39.269 --> 00:20:42.669
but potentially evidence that
could give rise to liability.

00:20:42.669 --> 00:20:46.390
Stop posting videos and
stop going on Twitter

00:20:46.390 --> 00:20:49.130
and lawyer up and talk
about what your rights are

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:51.660
before you start digging
yourself into a bigger hole

00:20:51.660 --> 00:20:52.780
than you already have.

00:20:52.780 --> 00:20:55.770
On the other hand, if I
was Jukin Media's attorney,

00:20:55.770 --> 00:20:58.020
which I'm not, this is not legal advice,

00:20:58.020 --> 00:20:59.610
I would probably tell them the same thing,

00:20:59.610 --> 00:21:01.720
that this is not something
you'd want to go to court over,

00:21:01.720 --> 00:21:04.120
they've lost on this before.

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:06.050
And that you're dealing with potentially

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:09.640
not only losing this
battle, not only in court,

00:21:09.640 --> 00:21:11.230
but in the court of public opinion,

00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:13.730
but potentially attorneys
fees are on the line.

00:21:13.730 --> 00:21:15.140
One of the things about copyright law

00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:18.340
is that sometimes the loser has to pay

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:21.370
the winners attorneys
fees, it gets really dicey.

00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:22.810
I don't know that this
is one of those cases,

00:21:22.810 --> 00:21:24.240
but it could be on the line.

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:27.050
But they're bad facts and
there's bad law for both sides.

00:21:27.050 --> 00:21:28.970
This is not something
that either side wants

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:29.890
to take to court.

00:21:29.890 --> 00:21:32.440
And the stakes are incredibly large.

00:21:32.440 --> 00:21:33.370
I talked about earlier,

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:36.610
that MxR might be on the
hook not just for the $6,000

00:21:36.610 --> 00:21:39.960
that Jukin requests,
but actually $600,000.

00:21:39.960 --> 00:21:42.640
The reason is that when you're
talking about copyright law,

00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:43.940
not only do you have to deal with

00:21:43.940 --> 00:21:45.280
what are called the actual damages,

00:21:45.280 --> 00:21:48.640
the out of pocket loss
that someone has suffered,

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:50.960
but you also have statutory damages.

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:52.110
And in some circumstances,

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:55.594
where you have a willful act
by the copyright infringer,

00:21:55.594 --> 00:22:00.594
you can get $150,000 per infringement.

00:22:00.610 --> 00:22:02.850
So those four different videos

00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:05.770
that may or may not have
constituted copyright infringement

00:22:05.770 --> 00:22:08.300
could mean that MxR was on the hook

00:22:08.300 --> 00:22:11.141
for $600,000 in statutory damages,

00:22:11.141 --> 00:22:12.690
that would be up to the court.

00:22:12.690 --> 00:22:15.140
On top of that, attorneys
fees are on the table as well,

00:22:15.140 --> 00:22:17.420
which might even be more than $600,000

00:22:17.420 --> 00:22:18.660
if this was taken to court,

00:22:18.660 --> 00:22:21.505
not to mention since we're
dealing with DMCA takedowns

00:22:21.505 --> 00:22:22.338
and YouTube strikes,

00:22:22.338 --> 00:22:23.740
that their channel might be deleted,

00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:26.140
which I assume is their
major source of income

00:22:26.140 --> 00:22:26.973
for the household.

00:22:26.973 --> 00:22:28.730
- It's just constantly in the fear

00:22:28.730 --> 00:22:30.440
of our channel being deleted.

00:22:30.440 --> 00:22:32.020
- So given the thousands,

00:22:32.020 --> 00:22:34.270
potentially millions of dollars at stake,

00:22:34.270 --> 00:22:36.540
is this extortion by Jukin Media

00:22:36.540 --> 00:22:39.746
against Henry and Jeannie at MxR?

00:22:39.746 --> 00:22:41.470
Well, almost certainly not,

00:22:41.470 --> 00:22:44.080
at least not the legal
definition of extortion.

00:22:44.080 --> 00:22:46.930
This might constitute the sort
of colloquial understanding

00:22:46.930 --> 00:22:50.730
of extortion, of bad guy
pushing a small person around,

00:22:50.730 --> 00:22:53.230
but it's almost certainly
not legal extortion.

00:22:53.230 --> 00:22:54.220
We'll talk about whether Jukin

00:22:54.220 --> 00:22:55.930
is a copyright troll in a second,

00:22:55.930 --> 00:23:00.330
but in general to be able to
form a claim for extortion

00:23:00.330 --> 00:23:03.571
under at least the federal
statute in 18 USC 1951,

00:23:03.571 --> 00:23:06.092
you have to have a wrongful claim.

00:23:06.092 --> 00:23:09.430
It has to be disconnected
from a claim of right.

00:23:09.430 --> 00:23:12.010
Jukin claims to have a good faith belief

00:23:12.010 --> 00:23:13.450
that this is copyright infringement.

00:23:13.450 --> 00:23:15.800
And on the facts as I see them,

00:23:15.800 --> 00:23:19.080
they could very well have a
meritorious copyright claim.

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:20.680
It's hard to say, it could go either way.

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:22.600
But because of that,
essentially, what they're doing

00:23:22.600 --> 00:23:24.100
is they're just making a demand,

00:23:24.100 --> 00:23:27.050
which potentially is legal against someone

00:23:27.050 --> 00:23:29.540
who was a potential copyright infringer.

00:23:29.540 --> 00:23:31.040
Jukin Media actually left a comment

00:23:31.040 --> 00:23:32.857
on MxR's YouTube channel, saying,

00:23:32.857 --> 00:23:34.787
"hi, we'd like the chance
to respond to this.

00:23:34.787 --> 00:23:36.227
"For starters, it's
important to understand

00:23:36.227 --> 00:23:37.777
"that we provide a service
for video creators.

00:23:37.777 --> 00:23:39.747
"We allow them to list
their content on our website

00:23:39.747 --> 00:23:42.448
"so that TV shows publishers,
YouTubers, influencers

00:23:42.448 --> 00:23:45.717
"or advertisers can buy a
license to use their content.

00:23:45.717 --> 00:23:47.977
"We have no ill will
towards you or your channel.

00:23:47.977 --> 00:23:49.737
"In effect, you're taking
other people's videos

00:23:49.737 --> 00:23:51.647
"without asking them then
posting them to your channel

00:23:51.647 --> 00:23:52.777
"and making money off of them.

00:23:52.777 --> 00:23:54.777
"We never want to issue copyright strikes,

00:23:54.777 --> 00:23:56.527
"we have a duty to do so to protect

00:23:56.527 --> 00:23:58.797
"the copyright of the creators
who have signed with us.

00:23:58.797 --> 00:24:00.487
"Again, there's no reason
we can't work together

00:24:00.487 --> 00:24:02.697
"so that you can have
access to amazing videos

00:24:02.697 --> 00:24:05.870
"and that creators of those
videos share in the economics."

00:24:05.870 --> 00:24:07.370
Of course, Jukin says they don't want

00:24:07.370 --> 00:24:09.020
to issue DMCA takedowns.

00:24:09.020 --> 00:24:09.853
And the reason is because

00:24:09.853 --> 00:24:11.540
there are actual legal repercussions

00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:14.570
for levying those DMCA takedown notices.

00:24:14.570 --> 00:24:16.460
So, of course, they don't want to do that

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:17.890
and they don't want to
take people to court

00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:20.420
because it's incredibly
expensive, and they might lose.

00:24:20.420 --> 00:24:21.883
But that's why some
people call Jukin Media

00:24:21.883 --> 00:24:22.840
a copyright troll.

00:24:22.840 --> 00:24:25.890
They get the copyrights
to various viral videos

00:24:25.890 --> 00:24:28.650
and then make demands for serious money

00:24:28.650 --> 00:24:32.010
from people that have used
those clips in other media.

00:24:32.010 --> 00:24:33.740
I reached out to both MxR and Jukin

00:24:33.740 --> 00:24:35.350
about this particular dispute.

00:24:35.350 --> 00:24:37.430
Jukin got back to me and
provided this statement,

00:24:37.430 --> 00:24:39.020
they say that they did consider fair use,

00:24:39.020 --> 00:24:40.910
but they feel that MxR's videos don't rise

00:24:40.910 --> 00:24:42.030
to the level of fair use

00:24:42.030 --> 00:24:45.350
and they point out that they
have paid out over $20 million

00:24:45.350 --> 00:24:47.540
to people whose videos they have licensed.

00:24:47.540 --> 00:24:50.470
But we don't know what
percentage of Jukin revenue comes

00:24:50.470 --> 00:24:51.570
from making these demands,

00:24:51.570 --> 00:24:54.520
versus proactively
licensing this material.

00:24:54.520 --> 00:24:57.310
And in some sense, having
middlemen like this

00:24:57.310 --> 00:24:59.136
can be a very good thing

00:24:59.136 --> 00:25:00.540
because it's often very, very hard

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:03.110
to know who owns the rights
to these various things.

00:25:03.110 --> 00:25:05.488
Someone owns the rights
to these viral clips,

00:25:05.488 --> 00:25:08.860
but often big businesses will not use them

00:25:08.860 --> 00:25:11.210
because they might not be able
to track down who owns them,

00:25:11.210 --> 00:25:13.690
and having a middleman, like Jukin

00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:15.100
can help solve that problem

00:25:15.100 --> 00:25:17.480
and create revenue from licensing

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:19.770
that otherwise simply wouldn't exist.

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:20.950
Now, if you want more detail about

00:25:20.950 --> 00:25:22.600
whether this constitutes extortion

00:25:22.600 --> 00:25:24.190
and the requirements for extortion,

00:25:24.190 --> 00:25:26.560
I recommend checking out
fellow YouTube lawyer,

00:25:26.560 --> 00:25:27.530
Richard Hoeg's channel,

00:25:27.530 --> 00:25:30.610
where he goes into a
lot of depth about this.

00:25:30.610 --> 00:25:33.080
Now, I disagree with him
about the dollar amounts

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:37.862
themselves being potential
evidence of a claim of extortion.

00:25:37.862 --> 00:25:41.650
Actually $1,500 per offense
is a pretty small amount

00:25:41.650 --> 00:25:42.483
if I'm honest.

00:25:42.483 --> 00:25:45.920
I know it can be a huge amount
to small YouTubers out there

00:25:45.920 --> 00:25:47.610
but in the world of copyright law,

00:25:47.610 --> 00:25:48.920
it's a very, very small amount

00:25:48.920 --> 00:25:52.580
and it is dwarfed by the
potential devastating effects

00:25:52.580 --> 00:25:55.800
of statutory damages or
an award of attorneys fees

00:25:55.800 --> 00:25:58.820
in an actual lawsuit over this case.

00:25:58.820 --> 00:26:00.600
One of the more famous extortion cases

00:26:00.600 --> 00:26:03.190
is the case of Autumn
Jackson and Bill Cosby.

00:26:03.190 --> 00:26:04.230
Autumn Jackson claimed

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:06.240
that she was the daughter of Bill Cosby

00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:09.180
before he was outed as a sexual predator

00:26:09.180 --> 00:26:10.767
and extorted him,

00:26:10.767 --> 00:26:13.970
said that she would ruin
his squeaky clean reputation

00:26:13.970 --> 00:26:15.190
in the news media

00:26:15.190 --> 00:26:19.190
by sowing these potentially
untrue allegations

00:26:19.190 --> 00:26:22.190
that he had fathered her out of wedlock.

00:26:22.190 --> 00:26:24.350
And one of the cases that
Richard Hoeg mentions

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:25.810
in his video about this case

00:26:25.810 --> 00:26:28.910
is the Michael Avenatti case, is ongoing.

00:26:28.910 --> 00:26:30.180
It hasn't led to a resolution.

00:26:30.180 --> 00:26:31.870
But one of the things
that Michael Avenatti

00:26:31.870 --> 00:26:33.360
is accused of having done

00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:37.394
is to extort a settlement out of Nike,

00:26:37.394 --> 00:26:39.090
not for his clients,

00:26:39.090 --> 00:26:42.500
but for a multi million dollar
consulting gig for himself.

00:26:42.500 --> 00:26:45.550
Lawyers are not allowed to
negotiate on their own behalf

00:26:45.550 --> 00:26:47.390
at the expense of their clients.

00:26:47.390 --> 00:26:50.960
And so that could conceivably
give rise to extortion.

00:26:50.960 --> 00:26:53.722
Michael Avenatti just
lost a motion to dismiss

00:26:53.722 --> 00:26:55.810
that legal claim.

00:26:55.810 --> 00:26:57.110
So it's going to go to trial.

00:26:57.110 --> 00:26:58.610
We'll see if he's convicted on that.

00:26:58.610 --> 00:27:02.099
But when a lawyer tries to
get something from a party

00:27:02.099 --> 00:27:04.710
that they're not legally
allowed to ask for,

00:27:04.710 --> 00:27:07.120
like a consulting gig
worth millions of dollars

00:27:07.120 --> 00:27:08.260
for their own behalf,

00:27:08.260 --> 00:27:10.640
that could potentially
give rise to extortion.

00:27:10.640 --> 00:27:13.650
Whereas here, mainly what's happening

00:27:13.650 --> 00:27:16.570
is that Jukin is asking for payment

00:27:16.570 --> 00:27:18.320
for copyright infringement.

00:27:18.320 --> 00:27:20.050
And as we've talked about before,

00:27:20.050 --> 00:27:21.930
whether copyright
infringement occurred or not,

00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:23.180
or whether it's fair use or not,

00:27:23.180 --> 00:27:24.820
is certainly an open question.

00:27:24.820 --> 00:27:26.831
By the way, some have said that YouTube

00:27:26.831 --> 00:27:27.664
should just shut this down.

00:27:27.664 --> 00:27:29.420
Jukin is abusing the copyright system,

00:27:29.420 --> 00:27:31.087
regardless of the underlying claims,

00:27:31.087 --> 00:27:33.900
and that YouTube should
just remove their ability

00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:35.300
to make these claims.

00:27:35.300 --> 00:27:38.290
Well, the problem is, this
isn't really YouTube's system.

00:27:38.290 --> 00:27:40.100
This is not content ID.

00:27:40.100 --> 00:27:43.170
What Jukin is talking about
here is issuing a DMCA takedown.

00:27:43.170 --> 00:27:46.380
This is written into the
Copyright Act section 512.

00:27:46.380 --> 00:27:48.660
If there is infringing
material on a website,

00:27:48.660 --> 00:27:49.520
including YouTube,

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:52.630
you are allowed to issue a DMCA notice

00:27:52.630 --> 00:27:54.260
and have it taken down.

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:56.480
YouTube has implemented a version of that

00:27:56.480 --> 00:28:00.020
which incurs a strike
when you get a DMCA claim.

00:28:00.020 --> 00:28:02.330
But there are also ways
to fight DMCA claims,

00:28:02.330 --> 00:28:04.360
which I've covered in my copyright course.

00:28:04.360 --> 00:28:06.400
So effectively YouTube
has to follow the law.

00:28:06.400 --> 00:28:07.900
When they get a DMCA claim,

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:10.710
they have to take the video
down, they can't ignore it

00:28:10.710 --> 00:28:13.710
and they can't stop people
from issuing DMCA claims.

00:28:13.710 --> 00:28:15.430
It's written into the copyright law

00:28:15.430 --> 00:28:17.670
that they just basically have to follow.

00:28:17.670 --> 00:28:20.500
And if Jukin does actually have a claim,

00:28:20.500 --> 00:28:23.690
then they have every right
to issue a DMCA takedown.

00:28:23.690 --> 00:28:26.430
Now, the Ninth Circuit
law under lens says that

00:28:26.430 --> 00:28:28.580
they have to have a good faith belief

00:28:28.580 --> 00:28:30.720
that they have a claim
of copyright infringement

00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:34.060
and they have taken into
consideration Fair Use

00:28:34.060 --> 00:28:36.670
before they have issued
that DMCA takedown.

00:28:36.670 --> 00:28:37.970
But look, if they have a claim,

00:28:37.970 --> 00:28:39.777
they have a right to issue a DMCA takedown

00:28:39.777 --> 00:28:41.000
and a copyright strike.

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:42.360
This is serious business.

00:28:42.360 --> 00:28:44.722
I get copyright claims all
the time on my channel,

00:28:44.722 --> 00:28:47.893
but I'm very confident
in my fair use because,

00:28:47.893 --> 00:28:49.720
number one, I have a law degree.

00:28:49.720 --> 00:28:51.927
I have over a decade of
experience as a trial lawyer

00:28:51.927 --> 00:28:53.470
and copyright lawyer.

00:28:53.470 --> 00:28:55.700
And I know what I'm talking about.

00:28:55.700 --> 00:28:57.730
The big businesses have lawyers

00:28:57.730 --> 00:28:59.730
who know this stuff inside and out

00:28:59.730 --> 00:29:03.140
because the operative word
here is that it is a business.

00:29:03.140 --> 00:29:04.950
And we've talked about the
copyright implications.

00:29:04.950 --> 00:29:06.250
But let's talk about these sort of,

00:29:06.250 --> 00:29:08.120
business law related things here.

00:29:08.120 --> 00:29:10.730
And even though I am not MxR lawyer,

00:29:10.730 --> 00:29:12.130
and this is #notlegaladvice,

00:29:13.010 --> 00:29:15.430
what I can say to MxR
is you need a lawyer.

00:29:15.430 --> 00:29:18.120
This is a business and
you can't go around,

00:29:18.120 --> 00:29:19.542
just floundering around,

00:29:19.542 --> 00:29:21.960
thinking that you're going to be safe.

00:29:21.960 --> 00:29:23.170
I asked my friend Roberto Blake,

00:29:23.170 --> 00:29:25.760
who knows the business of
YouTube better than everyone

00:29:25.760 --> 00:29:27.010
what he thought about this situation

00:29:27.010 --> 00:29:27.980
and here's what he said.

00:29:27.980 --> 00:29:30.610
- I think when you are a
full time content creator,

00:29:30.610 --> 00:29:33.460
when you have a very significant channel,

00:29:33.460 --> 00:29:35.910
like in this situation, that you have

00:29:35.910 --> 00:29:37.910
to really think of things like a business

00:29:37.910 --> 00:29:41.740
and you have to deal with
Jukin Media like a business.

00:29:41.740 --> 00:29:43.960
They aren't really gonna care too much

00:29:43.960 --> 00:29:46.110
about the court of public opinion.

00:29:46.110 --> 00:29:48.780
This isn't like a YouTube drama situation,

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:51.480
where calling them out,
really is that meaningful.

00:29:51.480 --> 00:29:55.880
YouTubers tend to get
very invested emotionally

00:29:55.880 --> 00:29:57.140
in these situations,

00:29:57.140 --> 00:29:59.420
when the real answer is to step back,

00:29:59.420 --> 00:30:02.610
treat it like a business
and call your lawyer

00:30:02.610 --> 00:30:03.920
and let them deal with it

00:30:03.920 --> 00:30:07.338
because this is a business
to business situation

00:30:07.338 --> 00:30:09.943
and you really want to
play your best cards.

00:30:09.943 --> 00:30:13.041
And when you take the step of just trying

00:30:13.041 --> 00:30:15.976
to out them in social media
or do something like that,

00:30:15.976 --> 00:30:18.958
you're playing more the
game like a YouTuber

00:30:18.958 --> 00:30:21.680
than like an entrepreneur
and a business person

00:30:21.680 --> 00:30:23.850
that's trying to protect
what they've built.

00:30:23.850 --> 00:30:24.890
- And that's exactly right.

00:30:24.890 --> 00:30:26.850
There are huge consequences here.

00:30:26.850 --> 00:30:29.260
And you have to lawyer up either way.

00:30:29.260 --> 00:30:31.440
MxR says that they don't have a lawyer.

00:30:31.440 --> 00:30:33.100
Well, they need to get one.

00:30:33.100 --> 00:30:35.340
And Jeannie says that she
spent half an hour going

00:30:35.340 --> 00:30:36.310
through Jukin videos.

00:30:36.310 --> 00:30:38.830
Well, number one, that is not enough time.

00:30:38.830 --> 00:30:41.216
And number two, you can't unring the bell.

00:30:41.216 --> 00:30:44.500
Some have argued that look, Jukin admits

00:30:44.500 --> 00:30:46.790
that the licence fee is only $50,

00:30:46.790 --> 00:30:49.260
why can't MxR just pay $200,

00:30:49.260 --> 00:30:51.288
$50 for each video,

00:30:51.288 --> 00:30:54.120
and then everyone can
go on their merry way?

00:30:54.120 --> 00:30:55.770
Well, the problem is that you're dealing

00:30:55.770 --> 00:30:56.650
with copyright here.

00:30:56.650 --> 00:30:58.600
Once you have committed
copyright infringement,

00:30:58.600 --> 00:31:00.120
and I'm not saying that MxR

00:31:00.120 --> 00:31:01.870
has committed copyright infringement

00:31:01.870 --> 00:31:04.470
or that they can't use
the Fair Use defense.

00:31:04.470 --> 00:31:06.050
They may not have committed infringement,

00:31:06.050 --> 00:31:07.730
and they may have that defense.

00:31:07.730 --> 00:31:09.300
If you infringe the copyright,

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:11.750
you can't then go back
through the database

00:31:11.750 --> 00:31:14.920
and buy the license,
that's simply not enough.

00:31:14.920 --> 00:31:17.390
There's statutory fees,
which are potentially huge,

00:31:17.390 --> 00:31:19.173
and it costs money to
get lawyers involved.

00:31:19.173 --> 00:31:21.849
So once the horse is out of the barn,

00:31:21.849 --> 00:31:23.650
you can't put it back in again.

00:31:23.650 --> 00:31:24.800
If you steal a car,

00:31:24.800 --> 00:31:27.640
you can't avoid jail time by
giving it back to the owner.

00:31:27.640 --> 00:31:29.860
And it seems the MxR has
already dug themselves

00:31:29.860 --> 00:31:32.840
further into the hole
by not having a lawyer.

00:31:32.840 --> 00:31:36.080
They said that they already
paid $2,000 to Jukin,

00:31:36.080 --> 00:31:37.950
related to two different videos,

00:31:37.950 --> 00:31:38.930
and then Jukin came back.

00:31:38.930 --> 00:31:42.680
Well, when you feed the trolls
they come back for more.

00:31:42.680 --> 00:31:45.610
- Clearly, I realized our
mistake was paying the first time

00:31:45.610 --> 00:31:46.610
because now they're like,

00:31:46.610 --> 00:31:48.840
we have we have someone
we could just easily

00:31:48.840 --> 00:31:50.500
just get easy money out of.

00:31:50.500 --> 00:31:53.090
- A competent lawyer probably
would have made Jukin sign

00:31:53.090 --> 00:31:55.010
a release of all claims,

00:31:55.010 --> 00:31:58.210
so that MxR wouldn't admit
liability and it would make sure

00:31:58.210 --> 00:32:00.256
that Jukin couldn't come back for more

00:32:00.256 --> 00:32:03.000
after they paid the $2,000.

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:04.820
But because they didn't sign a release,

00:32:04.820 --> 00:32:07.720
Jukin can come back over
on adjudicated claims,

00:32:07.720 --> 00:32:09.450
which is exactly what Jukin did.

00:32:09.450 --> 00:32:12.020
Additionally, a lawyer would
have negotiated it down.

00:32:12.020 --> 00:32:14.527
Jukin has basically admitted
that a reasonable license

00:32:14.527 --> 00:32:17.130
for these clips is $49,

00:32:17.130 --> 00:32:21.870
which would mean that an MxR
should pay $150, not $6,000.

00:32:21.870 --> 00:32:23.100
But the problem is that

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:24.600
because they didn't have any foresight,

00:32:24.600 --> 00:32:25.433
they didn't have a lawyer,

00:32:25.433 --> 00:32:26.300
they didn't know what they were doing

00:32:26.300 --> 00:32:27.500
when it came to copyright,

00:32:27.500 --> 00:32:28.960
they could be liable for thousands more,

00:32:28.960 --> 00:32:31.210
not just the $6,000 that Jukin claims,

00:32:31.210 --> 00:32:33.210
but potentially hundreds
of thousands of dollars

00:32:33.210 --> 00:32:34.820
in statutory fees.

00:32:34.820 --> 00:32:36.320
Also a word of caution to MxR,

00:32:36.320 --> 00:32:38.878
MxR has already linked to a video

00:32:38.878 --> 00:32:42.900
that takes MxR's side by a
channel called TheQuartering.

00:32:42.900 --> 00:32:46.580
I'd be very careful about
listening to that advice.

00:32:46.580 --> 00:32:50.130
- I strongly suggest MxR stand
up and tell them to get bent

00:32:50.130 --> 00:32:51.340
and tell him to sue them.

00:32:51.340 --> 00:32:53.903
And when they issue a copyright
strike on the channel,

00:32:53.903 --> 00:32:58.160
I would dispute it and I
would say take me to court.

00:32:58.160 --> 00:33:02.050
I don't think they're willing
to do that as a company,

00:33:02.050 --> 00:33:03.170
but it's not my money.

00:33:03.170 --> 00:33:06.150
- I'm sorry if this sounds
harsh, but Jukin claim

00:33:06.150 --> 00:33:08.560
isn't necessarily facially invalid.

00:33:08.560 --> 00:33:10.810
This is a case that could go either way.

00:33:10.810 --> 00:33:12.870
What MxR has done might be fair use,

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:14.830
but it also might not be fair use.

00:33:14.830 --> 00:33:16.620
It would be up to a judge or jury,

00:33:16.620 --> 00:33:18.210
and reasonable minds can differ.

00:33:18.210 --> 00:33:20.550
That's what happens when you go to court.

00:33:20.550 --> 00:33:21.880
You kind of roll the dice.

00:33:21.880 --> 00:33:24.830
Certainly lawyers who I've
talked to about this very case

00:33:24.830 --> 00:33:26.870
have differing ideas about it.

00:33:26.870 --> 00:33:29.810
And I would love love to tell you that MxR

00:33:29.810 --> 00:33:33.130
is clearly in the right and
Jukin is clearly in the wrong.

00:33:33.130 --> 00:33:35.080
I hate the way that they've gone about,

00:33:35.080 --> 00:33:37.780
trying to get money from MxR,

00:33:37.780 --> 00:33:40.360
but I don't know that that's the case.

00:33:40.360 --> 00:33:43.310
I love creators and I want
them to create more art,

00:33:43.310 --> 00:33:45.800
but we all have to operate
within the confines

00:33:45.800 --> 00:33:48.030
of the way the law actually is,

00:33:48.030 --> 00:33:49.850
not the way that we want it to be.

00:33:49.850 --> 00:33:54.660
And it's possible, maybe even
probable that MxR is wrong.

00:33:54.660 --> 00:33:57.330
And MxR isn't quite as
small as they claim,

00:33:57.330 --> 00:33:59.746
they can't stick their head in the sand.

00:33:59.746 --> 00:34:03.850
Trolls like this go out and
they look for deep pockets.

00:34:03.850 --> 00:34:07.101
The thing is, Jukin has
taken this to court before

00:34:07.101 --> 00:34:09.420
and effectively they've won.

00:34:09.420 --> 00:34:10.730
We don't know the actual outcome

00:34:10.730 --> 00:34:12.682
because there was a settlement

00:34:12.682 --> 00:34:15.990
but odds are Jukin got the
better of that particular case.

00:34:15.990 --> 00:34:19.440
And even winning can be losing
in a situation like this.

00:34:19.440 --> 00:34:20.310
Vindicating your rights

00:34:20.310 --> 00:34:23.000
could easily take half a million
dollars in attorneys fees,

00:34:23.000 --> 00:34:25.870
just ask Ethan Kline from h3h3,

00:34:25.870 --> 00:34:28.020
it took hundreds of thousands of dollars,

00:34:28.020 --> 00:34:30.669
They went through $200,000 in one month

00:34:30.669 --> 00:34:33.350
to ultimately be
successful in their claim.

00:34:33.350 --> 00:34:36.090
So don't assume that you can
start a GoFundMe over that.

00:34:36.090 --> 00:34:38.439
Jukin doesn't care about
what you think about it.

00:34:38.439 --> 00:34:40.940
A judge doesn't care
about your Twitter clout

00:34:40.940 --> 00:34:42.430
or bad Yelp reviews.

00:34:42.430 --> 00:34:46.550
So just be very careful about
whose advice you listen to.

00:34:46.550 --> 00:34:49.610
And again, the best thing you
can do is talk to an attorney.

00:34:49.610 --> 00:34:51.630
And I've represented clients on both sides

00:34:51.630 --> 00:34:52.670
of the copyright coin.

00:34:52.670 --> 00:34:55.170
I've represented clients
who have had to defend

00:34:55.170 --> 00:34:57.430
against meritless claims of infringement,

00:34:57.430 --> 00:34:58.770
and I've also represented clients

00:34:58.770 --> 00:35:01.020
who have had their works stolen by others

00:35:01.020 --> 00:35:02.410
and had to defend their rights.

00:35:02.410 --> 00:35:05.440
It's always a big deal
and it's always messy.

00:35:05.440 --> 00:35:06.620
And as Roberto Blake said,

00:35:06.620 --> 00:35:08.620
they need to think about
their YouTube channel

00:35:08.620 --> 00:35:09.840
as a business.

00:35:09.840 --> 00:35:13.270
MxR plays gets 900,000
views on average per day,

00:35:13.270 --> 00:35:15.450
sometimes well over a million views a day.

00:35:15.450 --> 00:35:18.400
At a conservative CPM of
$6 with the YouTube split,

00:35:18.400 --> 00:35:21.300
if monetized their channel
would get roughly $2,500

00:35:21.300 --> 00:35:23.330
to $3,000 per day,

00:35:23.330 --> 00:35:25.640
that equates to a
million dollars per year.

00:35:25.640 --> 00:35:26.620
That's not a small business,

00:35:26.620 --> 00:35:29.960
that is a medium sized business,
and they need a lawyer.

00:35:29.960 --> 00:35:31.320
Now I understand that
there's a controversy

00:35:31.320 --> 00:35:33.790
about whether their YouTube
channel is monetized or not.

00:35:33.790 --> 00:35:36.162
But I also understand that they have 10s

00:35:36.162 --> 00:35:38.140
of thousands of patrons on Patreon.

00:35:38.140 --> 00:35:41.350
And at a minimum, if they're
paying one to $2 per month,

00:35:41.350 --> 00:35:43.330
that means that they're
earning potentially

00:35:43.330 --> 00:35:44.940
a quarter of a million dollars per year

00:35:44.940 --> 00:35:46.640
just on Patreon alone.

00:35:46.640 --> 00:35:47.820
You can't get away with thinking

00:35:47.820 --> 00:35:50.430
that this is just mere YouTube drama.

00:35:50.430 --> 00:35:51.530
This is real life.

00:35:51.530 --> 00:35:53.380
The stakes are incredibly high.

00:35:53.380 --> 00:35:56.080
There are hundreds of thousands
of dollars at stake here.

00:35:56.080 --> 00:35:57.490
You might end up in federal court

00:35:57.490 --> 00:35:59.800
and this is not just a
word of warning to MxR,

00:35:59.800 --> 00:36:02.620
but this is a warning to
anyone with a YouTube channel.

00:36:02.620 --> 00:36:04.160
MxR says that they're worried

00:36:04.160 --> 00:36:06.532
they might have to stop reacting to videos

00:36:06.532 --> 00:36:08.180
and that they might just
react to pictures instead.

00:36:08.180 --> 00:36:11.180
Number one, if you are doing
things in a fair use way,

00:36:11.180 --> 00:36:13.410
then there's no need to stop.

00:36:13.410 --> 00:36:15.410
But I think they need to ask themselves

00:36:15.410 --> 00:36:17.250
whether they are very, very certain

00:36:17.250 --> 00:36:19.890
that they have met the
Fair Use requirement.

00:36:19.890 --> 00:36:21.630
- And at this point, it's like,

00:36:21.630 --> 00:36:25.230
I feel like we can't even
watch any videos anymore.

00:36:25.230 --> 00:36:27.890
We can't react to any videos period.

00:36:27.890 --> 00:36:32.280
- Like we can only look
at images and pictures.

00:36:32.280 --> 00:36:34.610
- And I will say that it is just a myth

00:36:34.610 --> 00:36:36.210
that they could forego the videos

00:36:36.210 --> 00:36:38.630
and start reacting to pictures instead,

00:36:38.630 --> 00:36:40.300
because pictures are copyrighted as well

00:36:40.300 --> 00:36:42.560
and they might run into
exactly the same problems

00:36:42.560 --> 00:36:44.310
that they run into with video,

00:36:44.310 --> 00:36:46.110
which is one of the reasons I gave a talk

00:36:46.110 --> 00:36:48.770
called the 11 myths of
copyright on the internet.

00:36:48.770 --> 00:36:50.140
If you're a content creator,

